 1 big thing: Europe&#x27;s growing pains 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Shoshana Gordon/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 "European rearmament" is catchy, convenient, clean. 

 But the truth, across many capitals, and in the minds and hands of those building factories and weaponry, is far more complicated . Why it matters: The continent is at a crossroads. Unpredictability gnaws at the relationship to the west with the U.S., and stray Russian missiles and drones arrive on its doorstep to the east.

 While an influx of defense spending is a solid start, many bottlenecks remain, entrenched by regulatory, political, geographical and cultural differences. Driving the news: This dynamic, and these observations, became clear over the course of several recent interviews, sit-downs and catch-ups, including on Zoom, at an embassy and in the twilight hours of a Washington defense conference .

 What they're saying: "Europe no longer lacks money; money isn't the problem. It lacks the ability, I would say, to turn the money into delivered capability quickly," Tobias Billström, the former Swedish foreign affairs minister now at Nordic Air Defense, told me.

 "We lack factories and we lack skilled workers and we lack procurement," he said. "We have to think about what we mean when we talk about 'European independence' or 'European sovereignty.' It's one thing to use these words. But the reality shows a different picture." Zoom in: A Morningstar report published last month found that defense markets are in the initial stage of "a new supercycle" and that European rearmament is a "long-term demand story," as "decades of underinvestment" have left militaries wanting.

 Yes, but: The same report warned that the buildup "faces an uneven path" ahead.

 Some countries, like Poland and Latvia, already exceed NATO's 3.5% spending benchmark . Many others don't. (Spain last summer rejected the alliance's spending increase as unreasonable, angering President Trump.) "Four years ago, defense topics as a whole were much more difficult to get understood by citizens," Hervé Dammann, a Thales executive vice president, told me. "The trajectory is there." Threat level: War in Ukraine and across the Middle East has forced a rethink of what frontline fighting looks like and what allies abroad are actually capable of. 

 What was once effective no longer works; what was once cordial is now confrontational. The bottom line: "I think people are saying the right things," but there "is less action than there are words," Joakim Sjöblom, the co-founder of SWEBAL , a TNT-making startup, told me.

 "To add another shift in your factory, it's quite low-hanging fruit, and most of the incumbents of industry have already done that. But to raise a new plant, within the bureaucracy of Europe, takes years," he said. "Finding the innovation is not necessarily the bottleneck, but reaching industrial manufacturing capacity is really hard." Go deeper: Ukraine's Starlink woes spur Europe to look for Musk alternatives 

 
 
