 2. Exclusive: Meet Corridian 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Eniola Odetunde/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Corridian, a New England-based robo-submarine maker, exited stealth this morning alongside word of its $38 million seed.

 The big picture: The startup wants to build the "stealth logistics layer for the undersea," co-founder Mike Guay told me, as "cheap, swarming one-way attack drones and long-range missiles" make the "surface and sky dangerous places to operate."

 Its advisers include naval heavyweights, retired Adm. Lisa Franchetti , retired Vice Adm. Jeffrey Trussler and retired Rear Adm. Milton "Jamie" Sands III among them. The latest: Corridian plans to unveil its flagship ultra-large autonomous undersea vehicle next year.

 It's designed to carry ISO-standard, containerized payloads long distances without a crew. (That could mean sensors or supplies. That could also mean weapons.) "I'd say our platform carries whatever payloads our customers choose. It's a platform in the truest sense," said Guay, a former Army field artillery officer. He declined to share additional specs, including how the AUV would be launched. Friction point: U.S. shipbuilding is in a tough spot . Many of America's highest-priority warships are behind schedule.

 Corridian, though, is adopting "more of an aerospace manufacturing model," according to Guay. That means the startup won't "interfere with the critical work of the submarine industrial base, while also leveraging a broad commercial supply chain," he said. "We have railroads to move over land. We have shipping to move on the surface. We have rockets into space. But undersea, nothing like that really exists." Follow the money: Point72 Ventures led the seed, with participation from Balerion Space Ventures, Harpoon Ventures, DCVC and Marlinspike.

 "I've invested in more than 25 companies, and I struggle to think of a more serious, elite team than the founders of Corridian," Stephen Rodriguez, an investor with DCVC, told me. "I backed them from the start because when the shooting starts, anything observable is gone." What's next: Corridian expects to double its workforce over the next year.

