 3. Taiwan at TechNet 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Annelise Capossela/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 U.S. Army Pacific is preparing for a potential Chinese invasion of Taiwan in 2027 and beyond, aligning with what has for years been known as the Davidson window.

 Why it matters: The Beijing-Taipei relationship is among the most closely watched in Washington — and could quickly devolve into a bloodbath.

 Driving the news: USARPAC commander Gen. Ronald Clark was asked last week about military readiness in the Indo-Pacific and if the Davidson window was still valid.

 "The answer for us at USARPAC is 'yes,'" he said, onstage at the TechNet Indo-Pacific conference in Hawai'i. "Again, it's not a date that we think conflict will happen — it is a period of time for which we're snapping the chalk line to say we need to be ready by," he added. "We'll continue to prepare. We'll continue to modernize. We'll continue to posture ourselves for the business of deterring war well beyond '27." Context: China regards Taiwan as a renegade province and seeks control over the self-governing island.

 The Davidson window, meanwhile, is named for retired Adm. Phil Davidson, the former head of Indo-Pacific Command, who in 2021 warned lawmakers of Beijing's buildup and ambitions . The intrigue: Clark was Davidson's chief of staff during the now-hallowed exchange.

 "We didn't know it was the Davidson window; somebody else came up with that," Clark said. "But I was, literally, in the headquarters with him, and part of the team that helped prepare for the testimony." Friction point: The Trump administration has previously held up arms deliveries to Taiwan.

